<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Danishmend Gazi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1084</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Danishmend Ahmad Gazi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Danishmend Gazi was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> leader who founded the Danishmendid principality in central and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> after the Seljuk victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> in 1071, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokat">Tokat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasya</a>. His exploits against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> inspired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendname">Danishmendname</a>, a Turkish heroic epic. His dynasty rivalled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of Rum until its conquest in the 1170s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/danishmend-gazi.jpg" alt="Danishmend Gazi" width="291" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dani%C5%9Fmendgazi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
